<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Miani</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1843">1843</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Miani was fought on 17 February 1843 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad-sindh">Hyderabad</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>, where a small British-Indian army under General Sir Charles Napier defeated the much larger forces of the Talpur <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">amirs</a> of Sindh. Napier won again at Dubba in March, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> annexed Sindh, which was attached to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> Presidency.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-miani.jpg" alt="Battle of Miani" width="320" height="207" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Edward Armitage · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edward_Armitage_(1817-96)_-_The_Battle_of_Meeanee,_17_February_1843_-_RCIN_407185_-_Royal_Collection.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>According to the Sindhi writer Nadeem Wagan, the main causes of the battle were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>'s wish to expand its possessions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a> and the ambitions of General Charles Napier, who had earlier been Governor of the Greek island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kefalonia">Kefalonia</a>. The Talpur kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>, loosely governed by its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">Amirs</a>, was an easier target than the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> kingdom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>. Napier marched from the Bombay Presidency across the Sindh border, and negotiations with the Talpur Amir in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad-sindh">Hyderabad</a> ended in an agreement after the Amir made significant concessions.</p><p>Napier then began to move back towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">Amir</a> disbanded the army he had mobilised. Napier, however, turned suddenly towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad-sindh">Hyderabad</a>, citing hostile intentions by the Amir, and marched on the capital at speed. The Talpur army, raised largely from volunteers in time of war, could not reassemble in full: a second force of about 8,000 under Mir Sher Muhammad Talpur failed to arrive in time, and Napier had won over the Amir of Khairpur, Mir Ali Murad Talpur, with promises of land and titles.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The numbers on each side are disputed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> later put its own force at about 2,800, while Talpur records described armies of roughly equal size, around 8,000 to 10,000 each. The Company's British soldiers and Indian sepoys carried Brown Bess muskets and had modern artillery and a supply train. The Talpur forces, commanded by Mir Jan Muhammad Khanani, were mostly cavalry armed with muskets, spears and swords, with some old guns acquired from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>.</p><p>In four to five hours of fighting, repeated cavalry charges were cut down by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musket</a> and artillery fire; where they reached the British lines, Napier wrote in his Conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> that he had to ride among his troops to keep them from falling back. In the Sindhi history Jannat ul Sindh, Mir Jan Muhammad was killed, a cannonball blew up the Talpur ammunition store, and Mir Nasir Khan Talpur left the field with 1,200 cavalry for the Fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad-sindh">Hyderabad</a>. Estimates of the Talpur dead run from about 5,000 to 6,000; the Company's paymasters recorded 256 British casualties, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baloch">Baloch</a> accounts 3,000 dead.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>On 24 March 1843 Mir Sher Muhammad Khan Talpur, later called Sher-e-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>, reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad-sindh">Hyderabad</a> with about 8,000 men and gave Napier forty-eight hours to leave the fort. Napier, reinforced from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a>, answered with artillery and defeated him at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hyderabad-1843">Battle of Dubba</a>. Sher Muhammad sought help in vain from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and the Khan of Kalat, and surrendered ten years later under an amnesty. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">amirs</a> of Hyderabad were exiled to the Andaman Islands, and the battle honour Meeanee is borne by the 22nd (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheshire">Cheshire</a>) Regiment and several Indian regiments.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Miani” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the British conquered Sindh in 1843 after the <b>Battle of Miani</b>, Mir Ali Murad Khan of Khairpur had sided with them and kept his state, though he later lost land after being accused of forging a treaty.</blockquote><a href="/state/khairpur-state">Khairpur State</a></li><li><blockquote>Charles Napier defeated the Talpurs at <b>Miani</b> and Dubbo in 1843, annexing Sindh to the Bombay Presidency.</blockquote><a href="/state/talpur-dynasty">Talpur dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
